\subsection{Controlled Contamination}
\label{sec:ctrl_contam}

We validate $\ell_z^*$ on MMLU with ground-truth contamination.
Three clean seeds yield $\ell_z^*$ values of $s_1{=}8.01$, $s_2{=}5.94$, $s_3{=}8.08$ (mean$\,{=}\,7.35\pm1.21$); $s_2$ is ${\sim}26\%$ lower than $s_1$/$s_3$, attributable to seed-specific training dynamics (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_sensitivity}).
All five conditions achieve comparable accuracy on uncontaminated items (clean $\approx$ 0.679), while contaminated models show accuracy gains on seen items (0.731 at 15\%, 0.764 at 50\%).

\paragraph{Finding 1: Detection signal.}
Both contamination levels produce $\ell_z^*$ shifts far exceeding clean-seed variability.
At 15\% contamination, $|\text{Cohen's}\;d|{=}3.58$; at 50\%, $|d|{=}1.56$ (ddof$\,{=}\,1$, $n{=}3$ clean seeds).
The contamination signal exceeds the clean-clean $\Delta\ell_z^*$ noise floor (0.70) by a factor of 5.7$\times$.

\paragraph{Finding 2: Free-$\theta$ non-monotonicity.}
Under free-$\theta$ estimation, dose-response is non-monotonic: $\Delta\ell_z^*{=}{-}4.34$ at 15\% versus ${-}1.90$ at 50\%.
The 50\% condition produces a \emph{weaker} signal than 15\% because contamination inflates $\hat{\theta}_{\text{MLE}}$, which absorbs part of the aberrance into a higher ability estimate ($\Delta\theta{=}0.30$ at 15\%, $0.47$ at 50\%).

\paragraph{Finding 3: Fixed $\theta$ restores monotonicity.}
Anchoring at the pre-contamination baseline ($\theta_{\text{fixed}}{=}{-}0.097$, mean of $n{=}3$ clean $\hat{\theta}$ estimates) restores monotonic dose-response: $\Delta\ell_z^*_{\text{fixed}}{=}2.68$ (15\%) and $8.36$ (50\%), a ratio of 3.12 (Figure~\ref{fig:ctrl_contam}, right panel).

\paragraph{Finding 4: Memorization fingerprint.}
Splitting by seen/unseen items reveals a characteristic fingerprint: at 15\% contamination, memorized items yield $\ell_z^*{\approx}0$ (consistent with the model's inflated $\hat{\theta}$), while unseen items produce $\ell_z^*{=}3.1$, exposing genuine aberrance on the non-memorized portion.

\paragraph{Finding 5: Memorization fraction.}
The fraction of contaminated items that the model memorizes is 0.38 (15\%) and 0.29 (50\%).
SFT generalizes beyond the memorized items, as fewer than half the injected items show rote recall.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_3_ctrl_contam.pdf}
  \caption{Controlled contamination on MMLU. \textbf{Left:} $\ell_z^*$ distributions for clean ($n{=}3$ seeds) and contaminated conditions. \textbf{Center:} Free-$\theta$ versus fixed-$\theta$ $\Delta\ell_z^*$ across contamination levels, illustrating the $\theta$-absorption effect. \textbf{Right:} Seen/unseen item split at 15\% contamination. $\theta_{\text{fixed}}$ computed as mean of $n{=}3$ clean seed $\hat{\theta}$ estimates ($\theta_{\text{fixed}}{=}{-}0.097$).}
  \label{fig:ctrl_contam}
\end{figure*}

